+\begingroup\small
+\section*{S22. Separate follow-up: per-view projection of guidance interaction}
+This post-submission follow-up is separate from the TCAS/C3 study. It tests a projection-based intervention on a different decomposition of the denoiser prediction; it does not modify the GeoTex residual scale and is not a TCAS replication. Projection-based guidance and conflict projection have prior precedents, including Adaptive Projected Guidance (APG) and PCGrad~\cite{apg2025,pcgrad2020}. The experiment below is reported as a task-specific follow-up, not as a claim that projection itself is new.
+
+For a matched latent and denoising step, let $f_u,f_c$ denote the unconditional and conditional predictions with geometry, and $b_u,b_c$ their counterparts without the geometry condition. At guidance scale $s=2$, define the geometry-related guided difference $g=[f_u+s(f_c-f_u)]-[b_u+s(b_c-b_u)]$ and the no-geometry conditional difference $a=b_c-b_u$. The latter is a condition difference, not a pure appearance gradient. For each of the six view blocks in the packed latent, the tested view-wise projection adds back only the component of $a$ that is negatively aligned with $g$:
+\begin{equation}
+\widehat{f}=f_u+s(f_c-f_u)-\frac{\min(\langle g,a\rangle,0)}{\max(\|a\|_2^2,10^{-12})}a.
+\end{equation}
+The six blocks are defined by the model's $3\times2$ packed-view layout. Grid projection uses one block for the complete latent; interaction projection uses a separate conditional decomposition. The code, cohort freeze, 768-cell identity ledger, and score rows are supplied in \texttt{evidence/round012/confirmation64/}.
+
+The cohort comprises 64 objects excluded from the separate 32-object development cohort. It is not new to the broader research history: these object inputs were part of earlier Fresh C work. Four conditions (native, grid projection, view projection, and interaction projection) were generated with three seeds (42, 2026, 31415), 50 denoising steps, and CFG 2. Before generation, the two primary tests were fixed as view projection minus native on source-view FG-PSNR and CIEDE2000. The object is the resampling unit; seeds are averaged within object. We used paired object bootstrap intervals and two-sided sign-flip tests, with Holm correction across the two primaries.
+
+\begin{table}[ht]
+\centering\small
+\caption{Preregistered view-projection primaries on the 64-object follow-up cohort. Differences are view projection minus native; positive PSNR and negative CIEDE2000 favor projection.}
+\label{tab:s22-primary}
+\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
+\toprule
+Source-view endpoint & Mean difference & 95\% object CI & Objects improved & Holm-adjusted $p$ \\
+\midrule
+FG-PSNR (dB) & $+4.283$ & $[+3.409,+5.205]$ & 61/64 & $1.0\times10^{-5}$ \\
+CIEDE2000 & $-2.230$ & $[-3.061,-1.563]$ & 57/64 & $1.0\times10^{-5}$ \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+The descriptive nonreference-view results are mixed and do not establish noninferiority. View projection minus native gives FG-PSNR $-0.282$~dB (pointwise 95\% CI $[-0.672,+0.102]$), FG-LPIPS $+0.000269$ ($[-0.001533,+0.002073]$), CIEDE2000 $+0.425$ ($[-0.157,+1.042]$), and FG-SSIM $+0.01062$ ($[+0.00171,+0.01957]$). These secondary intervals are unadjusted; no noninferiority margin was specified. Thus the primary result concerns source-view image agreement only.
+
+\paragraph{Development comparisons with projection baselines.}
+On the separate 32-object, three-seed development cohort, view projection minus projection-only APG had nonreference FG-LPIPS difference $+0.000800$ (pointwise 95\% CI $[-0.002459,+0.004299]$); versus the APG paper-parameter preset evaluated at native CFG 2, the difference was $-0.000462$ ($[-0.004354,+0.003482]$). Interaction projection gave $-0.002070$ ($[-0.005351,+0.001259]$) and $-0.003331$ ($[-0.007565,+0.000593]$) against these two baselines. Holm-adjusted $p$ values across the four comparisons were 1.000, 1.000, 0.725, and 0.510, respectively. None establishes an advantage over APG; the preset is not the original paper's CFG-15 setting.
+
+\paragraph{Second-interface development comparison.}
+On 32 MV-Adapter objects, three seeds, and six native-camera embedded-baseColor target views, the all-six-view FG-LPIPS differences versus native were $+0.000184$ for grid projection (pointwise 95\% CI $[-0.018894,+0.011364]$), $-0.000522$ for view projection ($[-0.023768,+0.012842]$), and $-0.000864$ for interaction projection ($[-0.020489,+0.010455]$). The three prespecified candidate-versus-native Holm-adjusted $p$ values were all 1.000. This development-only endpoint uses the MV-Adapter feature-scaled interface and embedded baseColor, not registered source-reference fidelity or unseen baked appearance; eight legal constant-white targets remain in the full cohort, with the 24 textured objects only a descriptive subset.
+
+Together these follow-up results provide a bounded source-view comparison for one geometry-conditioned CFG interaction intervention. They do not establish view-general noninferiority, a baked-texture or PBR improvement, APG superiority, a cross-backbone effect, or any effect of TCAS/C3. The 64-object cohort also has historical Fresh C exposure. These records remain separate from the 300-object Fresh C TCAS study, the native MV-Adapter N=50 temporal-schedule study, the MVDiffusion N=75 panel, and the ten-object bake pilot.
+
+\begin{thebibliography}{2}
+\bibitem{apg2025}
+Sadat S, Hilliges O, Weber R M. Eliminating oversaturation and artifacts of high guidance scales in diffusion models. \textit{International Conference on Learning Representations}, 2025. arXiv:2410.02416.
+
+\bibitem{pcgrad2020}
+Yu T, Kumar S, Gupta A, Levine S, Hausman K, Finn C. Gradient surgery for multi-task learning. \textit{Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems}, 2020, 33: 5824--5836.
+\end{thebibliography}
+\endgroup
